<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Austrasia</h1><p class="st-meta">511 CE – 751 CE · capital Metz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Eastern kingdom of the Merovingian Franks, centred on Metz and Reims, and the homeland of the Carolingian dynasty.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Austrasia, the eastern land, arose from the share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>' eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-i">Theuderic I</a> in 511, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>. It covered north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and was extended over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-alamannia">Alemannia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its population was more Germanic than that of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neustria">Neustria</a>, and its aristocracy was powerful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a> and his Visigothic queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhild</a> fought a long feud with Neustria. From the 7th century the mayors of the palace, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippinids">Pippinid</a> family, held real power while kings reigned as figureheads.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a> defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neustria">Neustria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tertry">Tertry</a> in 687 and became master of the whole <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pippin the Short</a> followed, and in 751 Pippin made himself king, ending Austrasia as a separate kingdom.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/austrasia.jpg" alt="Austrasia" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankish_Empire_481_to_814-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>511 CE – 751 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Metz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Theuderic I, Sigebert I, Dagobert I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Frankish, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Metz, Kingdom of Reims</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=631&amp;focus=austrasia" aria-label="Austrasia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4823 464 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Austrasia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5143 720L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5143 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5143 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Metz. <a href="/atlas?year=631&amp;focus=austrasia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

